              examples) are read directly. Grep across manuals with
              search_sources using the source session names. Glob
              `pattern` filters entry names."
inputSchema   {"type": "object",
               "properties": {
                 "pattern": {"type": "string"}},
               "required": []}
annotations   {"readOnlyHint": true, "idempotentHint": true,
               "openWorldHint": false}
outputSchema  none. reply body: catalog sections in order, each
              entry one line —
                <name>  <title>  [source: <session>|plain|pdf only]
              plus a closing hint line naming doc_read.

not scope-filtered: doc entries are catalog items, not theories
(spec "scope note" — discovery is never scoped). scope_show /
resources/list are unaffected.

backing mechanism
-----------------

computed ONCE at server startup alongside the session-structure deps
object (same lifecycle, same rationale — pure parsing, no heaps):

1. catalog: Doc.contents(ML_Settings.init()) -> Contents(sections),
   each Section {title, important, entries}, Entry {name, path,
   title}. release_notes() and examples() entries have non-pdf
   paths (plain text); main_contents() entries are the manuals
   (path.pdf when no plain file exists).
2. source join: from the startup Sessions.Structure, the sessions
   in group "doc" (src/Doc is on the distribution's top-level
   ROOTS, so they are already enumerated — verified: session
   headers read `session Isar_Ref (doc) in "Isar_Ref" = HOL +`).
   for each, options.string("document_variants") gives the doc
   name(s); build map variant-name -> session-name. join:
   entry.name in map -> source session; entry.path non-pdf ->
   "plain"; neither -> "pdf only".
3. filtering: pattern is the same glob machinery list_sessions
   uses over entry names; no pattern = everything. unknown pattern
   -> empty listing, ok (probe-safe, it is a search).

user-extended catalogs come for free: Doc.dirs() reads
$ISABELLE_DOCS (colon-separated; each dir contributes via its own
Contents file), so a papers/ folder registered through a
component's etc/settings shows up with zero server code (spec,
"the catalog is user-extensible"). such entries normally join as
"plain" or "pdf only" (no doc session) — no special casing.

details to settle first
-----------------------

D1  document_variants parsing: the option value is a ":"-separated
    variant list, each variant "name" or "name=tags" (see
    Document_Build in Pure). the doc sessions all use a single bare
    name, but parse properly: split on ":", take the part before
    "=" of each, map EVERY variant name to the session (harmless
    for singletons, correct for multi-variant sessions).
Q1  where the catalog struct lives: mcp_session.scala already owns
    the startup structure/deps; a small Doc_Catalog object in
    mcp/src (own file or inside mcp_session.scala) keeping
    {sections, entry -> source resolution} — decide when writing,
    doc_read consumes the same object (its plan assumes it).
D2  Doc.contents needs ML_Settings (the examples section expands
    $ML_SOURCES): ML_Settings.init() at startup, once. if init
    proves awkward in the server context, degrade to
    release_notes() ++ main_contents() and drop examples — pin
    which in the implementation notes.

name/argument mapping across the layers
---------------------------------------

  mcp client edge   tools/call "doc_list", {"pattern": "isar*"}
  scala builtin row handler reads the memoized catalog, applies the
                    glob, renders the listing. NO backend.ir call,
                    NO protocol command — same shape as
                    list_sessions.

